Batch Processing with Spot Instances

Use Spot Instances for big data processing jobs to reduce cost.
Since Spot Instances can be interrupted, architect the batch job to checkpoint progress.
For example, a genomic data analysis that saves intermediate results, so when a Spot Instance is interrupted, you can resume on a new instance.
-------------------------------------

Example: Cost-Effective Batch Processing with AWS Batch Using Spot Instances
Scenario: You have a large set of independent data processing jobs (e.g., video rendering, genomic analysis, or log processing). You want to run these jobs in parallel on the cheapest compute possible.


How it works:
--------
Setup AWS Batch:
Define a Compute Environment that uses a mix of Spot Instances (e.g., c5.large, m5.large) with fallback to On-Demand instances if Spot capacity is unavailable.

Create a Job Queue linked to the compute environment.

Define a Job Definition describing the container image and resource requirements.

---------
Submit Batch Jobs:
You submit many independent jobs to the Batch queue.

AWS Batch schedules jobs on available Spot Instances automatically.

If a Spot Instance is interrupted (with a 2-minute warning), AWS Batch automatically reschedules the job on a new instance.


----------
Real Code Snippet Example (CLI commands):
bash
# 1. Create compute environment with Spot instances enabled
aws batch create-compute-environment \
  --compute-environment-name SpotComputeEnv \
  --type MANAGED \
  --state ENABLED \
  --compute-resources type=SPOT,minvCpus=0,maxvCpus=128,desiredvCpus=64,instanceTypes=c5.large,m5.large,spotIamFleetRole=arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/AmazonEC2SpotFleetRole,subnets=subnet-abc123,securityGroupIds=sg-0123456789abcdef0

# 2. Create a job queue pointing to the compute environment
aws batch create-job-queue \
  --job-queue-name SpotJobQueue \
  --state ENABLED \
  --priority 1 \
  --compute-environment-order order=1,computeEnvironment=SpotComputeEnv

# 3. Register a job definition
aws batch register-job-definition \
  --job-definition-name MyBatchJob \
  --type container \
  --container-properties '{"image":"my-docker-image","vcpus":2,"memory":4096}'

# 4. Submit a batch job to the queue
aws batch submit-job \
  --job-name MyBatchJobRun1 \
  --job-queue SpotJobQueue \
  --job-definition MyBatchJob \
  --container-overrides '{"command":["python","process_data.py","--input","file1.json"]}'

-------------------------
Benefits:
Up to 90% savings compared to On-Demand instances.

Automatic rescheduling on Spot interruption ensures fault tolerance.

Supports any batch workload that can checkpoint or tolerate retries.

Seamless integration with other AWS services like S3, SNS, CloudWatch Logs.

--------------------------
Use Case Example:
You have a data analytics job that processes chunks of large GPS datasets stored in S3. Using AWS Batch on Spot Instances:
You containerize your processing script.
Split input datasets into small files (e.g., file1.json, file2.json, etc.).
Submit a batch job per file; AWS Batch runs them in parallel on Spot.
If interruption happens, AWS Batch reschedules the interrupted jobs automatically.
Leverage CloudWatch Logs for monitoring job execution.
This approach drastically reduces your compute bill compared to using On-Demand EC2 instances, especially for large-scale or repetitive batch processing workloads.
